Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/13/new-apple-tv-4k-announced/">announced</a> the fourth-generation <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/apple-tv/">Apple TV</a> 4K earlier this week, introducing the A19 chip, built-in microphones for hands-free <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/siri/">Siri</a>, four-speaker <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/homepod/">HomePod</a> surround sound, and an updated &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; Remote. It replaces the third-generation model introduced in 2022, bringing a number of internal changes while retaining the same basic design and most of the same entertainment features.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/Apple-TV-4K-4th-Generation-feature4.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1070610" /><br/>
The new model costs &#36;299 and comes with 128GB of storage. &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; AI is also coming to the third-generation model, and the new remote can be purchased separately for existing devices. Apple says the assistant will be available in beta with tvOS 27.2 on second-generation and later &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; 4K models, including conversational entertainment searches, follow-up questions, and personal context through a compatible paired device.<br/>
<br/>
The new model's distinctive hardware benefit is direct voice input through the box's microphones; access to the voice assistant itself is shared. Whether you should upgrade therefore comes down to which hardware changes you will actually use.<br/>
