<h3 id="should_you_upgrade_from_the_third-generation_apple_tv_4k">Should You Upgrade From the Third-Generation Apple TV 4K?</h3><br/>
Most third-generation &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; 4K owners should keep their existing device. For ordinary streaming through television speakers, a soundbar, or an AV receiver, the new model does not add new capabilities. The maximum viewing resolution and HDR support remains the same. &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; AI's availability on the older hardware further reduces the need to replace a working device.<br/>
<br/>
The strongest reason to upgrade is a planned four-speaker &zwnj;HomePod&zwnj; system. If you already own two second-generation full-sized HomePods and want dedicated rear speakers, the new &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; enables a specific setup your existing model cannot provide. Include the cost of additional or replacement speakers when judging the upgrade.<br/>
<br/>
Hands-free control is another practical reason if you regularly use &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; and want to speak directly to the device. It is likely to be more useful when the &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; is directly in the viewing room than when it is installed in a separate equipment cupboard or rack.<br/>
<br/>
AV1 hardware decoding may justify the upgrade for a particular media library or playback workflow. Gaming enthusiasts may also value the newer GPU architecture, but the benefit depends on the games available for tvOS and how developers use the hardware. <br/>
<br/>
If locating the remote is a source of frustration, buying the &#36;59 &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; Remote separately costs &#36;240 less than replacing the entire &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj;. That purchase supplies the new remote features while retaining your existing device.<br/>
<br/>
<h3 id="should_you_buy_the_third-_or_fourth-generation_model">Should You Buy the Third- or Fourth-Generation Model?</h3><br/>
For a new purchase, the fourth-generation &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; 4K is the stronger choice when the price difference is modest or you intend to use its exclusive features. Compared with the older 128GB model's final &#36;249 Apple price, the &#36;50 premium is easier to justify than the much larger difference suggested by comparing original launch prices.<br/>
<br/>
That being said, a discounted third-generation model remains a sensible purchase. The older model is particularly appealing when the saving is substantial and you are not interested in a four-speaker &zwnj;HomePod&zwnj; sound system.<br/>
<br/>
